# QXL draft content registry. No patient data. Review every page before deployment.
# slug, title, direct answer, practical guidance, FAQ pairs, internal related slugs
PAGES = []
def add(slug,title,answer,guidance,faqs,related=''):
    PAGES.append(dict(slug=slug,title=title,answer=answer,guidance=guidance,faqs=faqs,related=related.split()))
add('sti-testing-after-unprotected-sex','STI Testing After Unprotected Sex in Bangalore','Testing after sex without a condom depends on the type of exposure, anatomical sites, symptoms, timing, vaccination and treatment history. Blood tests may assess HIV, syphilis and viral hepatitis; site-specific NAAT may detect gonorrhoea and chlamydia.','If possible HIV exposure occurred within 72 hours, seek urgent medical assessment for PEP. Do not wait for testing. If pregnancy is possible, seek timely emergency contraception advice. A clinician can decide whether baseline and follow-up testing are appropriate.', [('What should I do after unprotected sex yesterday?','Seek urgent medical advice for possible HIV PEP within 72 hours and pregnancy prevention when relevant; baseline tests do not exclude the recent exposure.'),('Can one sexual encounter cause an STI?','Yes, risk varies considerably by infection, contact and partner status. Symptoms alone cannot establish infection.'),('Which tests should I get after sex without a condom?','Depending on risk, consider HIV Ag/Ab, syphilis serology, HBV/HCV assessment and site-specific gonorrhoea/chlamydia NAAT.'),('Does washing or urinating after sex prevent STIs?','No. Washing or urinating does not reliably prevent STIs. Avoid irritating chemicals and unprescribed antibiotics.')],'condom-break-sti-testing sti-testing-window-periods sti-testing-after-oral-sex sti-testing-after-anal-sex confidential-sti-testing-bangalore')
add('condom-break-sti-testing','Condom Broke or Slipped During Sex – STI Testing and Urgent Advice','A condom breaking, slipping, leaking or being removed during sex may expose mucosal surfaces to infection. Ejaculation is not required for all STI transmission.','Consider prompt clinician assessment for HIV PEP and hepatitis B prevention, depending on exposure and vaccination. Pregnancy prevention may also be time-sensitive. Do not wait for symptoms.', [('What if the condom broke but there was no ejaculation?','Risk is not zero for every STI; discuss the actual contact and partner status with a clinician.'),('Is PEP needed after every condom break?','No. A clinician evaluates HIV risk and timing; if indicated, PEP should start as soon as possible within 72 hours.'),('Can I take an STI test the next day?','Baseline testing can help, but a negative result may not detect the new exposure. Follow-up may be needed.')],'sti-testing-after-unprotected-sex hiv-pep-prep-testing sti-testing-window-periods')
add('sti-testing-window-periods','STI Testing Window Periods – When to Test After Sexual Exposure','There is no single window period for all STIs. Symptoms, specimen type, test method, time since exposure and medicines affect the plan. HIV laboratory antigen/antibody tests using venous blood usually detect infection 18–45 days after exposure in the absence of antiretroviral medication.','A baseline test may be useful immediately. For symptoms, seek assessment now rather than waiting for an arbitrary interval. Follow-up testing is pathogen- and exposure-specific. Tests months or years later may detect current infection or lasting markers but cannot necessarily reconstruct a cleared infection.', [('Can I test after 24 hours, 3 days or a week?','Yes for baseline assessment or symptoms, but early negative results may require follow-up.'),('Can I test at 2 weeks or one month?','Yes. A clinician selects molecular and serological tests and repeat timing; no universal panel is definitive at these times.'),('Is testing useful at 3, 6 or 8 months or one year?','Yes, depending on exposure. Current infection and relevant serological markers can be assessed; previous cleared infections may not be detectable.'),('What is the difference between incubation and window period?','Incubation is time until symptoms; the diagnostic window is time until a test can detect infection.')],'hiv-testing-after-exposure understanding-sti-test-results sti-testing-after-unprotected-sex')
add('hiv-testing-after-exposure','Fourth-Generation HIV Testing After Exposure in Bangalore','A laboratory HIV-1/2 fourth-generation antigen/antibody test detects HIV-1 p24 antigen and antibodies to HIV-1/2. In people not taking antiretrovirals, venous laboratory Ag/Ab testing usually detects infection within 18–45 days; HIV NAT usually within 10–33 days.','A reactive screening result requires supplemental testing under the applicable diagnostic algorithm. PEP or PrEP can affect detection and follow-up. No HIV test detects infection immediately; do not use an isolated index value, symptoms, CBC or a percentage calculator to diagnose HIV.', [('Is HIV testing at 28, 30, 42 or 45 days reliable?','Interpret the result against the specific assay and exposure. If testing before the end of its window, follow-up may be indicated.'),('What about HIV test after 90 days, six months or a year?','A negative result after the applicable window without a new exposure is reassuring; antiretroviral use can change follow-up.'),('Can I have HIV without symptoms?','Yes. Symptoms are not a reliable way to diagnose or rule out HIV.'),('Does a reactive HIV screening test establish a diagnosis?','No. Supplemental testing and counselling are required.'),('What is HIV RNA PCR versus fourth-generation ECLIA?','NAT detects viral nucleic acid; Ag/Ab detects antigen and antibodies. The clinician selects the appropriate test.')],'hiv-pep-prep-testing sti-testing-window-periods understanding-sti-test-results')
add('hiv-pep-prep-testing','HIV PEP and PrEP – Urgent Care, Baseline and Follow-Up Testing','PEP is emergency HIV prevention after a potential exposure; seek medical assessment as soon as possible and within 72 hours. PrEP is prevention for people with ongoing risk. Neither is a substitute for prevention or testing for other STIs.','A clinician determines eligibility, prescribes medication, and orders appropriate baseline and follow-up HIV, hepatitis, renal and other tests. Do not delay PEP for laboratory results or self-medicate. If the 72-hour window has passed, obtain medical advice about testing and ongoing prevention.', [('Where can I get PEP urgently?','Visit an emergency department, qualified clinician or HIV service immediately. A routine laboratory appointment is not a substitute.'),('Can PEP prevent gonorrhoea or syphilis?','No. HIV PEP is directed at HIV.'),('When do I test after PEP or while taking PrEP?','Follow the clinician’s current protocol; HIV Ag/Ab and NAT may be indicated. Standard untreated windows cannot simply be applied.')],'sti-testing-after-unprotected-sex hiv-testing-after-exposure urgent-medical-support')
add('sti-testing-after-oral-sex','STI Screening After Oral Sex – Throat Swab PCR & Comprehensive Blood Testing','Oral exposure can transmit gonorrhoea, syphilis, herpes and other infections. HIV risk from oral sex alone is little to none. Throat gonorrhoea NAAT may be appropriate after pharyngeal exposure; some suitable dual-target assays also detect chlamydia.','Urine cannot exclude an infection confined to the throat. Routine pharyngeal chlamydia screening is generally not recommended. QXL lists extragenital screening; confirm the kit, targets, validated specimen, performing lab, collection location, fee and turnaround before collection. Blood and genital tests should be selected separately according to risk.', [('Can a throat swab test detect both gonorrhoea and chlamydia?','Some dual-target NAATs can detect both in a suitable pharyngeal sample. Ask QXL to confirm the exact assay and verification status.'),('Is the assay validated for throat swabs?','Ask for the specific platform, manufacturer specimen claim and laboratory verification. A urine-only assay cannot be assumed suitable.'),('Should I test after oral sex without symptoms?','Testing depends on which site was exposed and individual risk; infections may be asymptomatic.'),('Can I arrange HIV, RPR/TPHA, hepatitis B triple panel and throat PCR together?','Request an itemised quote and specimen-specific confirmation. HBsAg alone is not the triple panel; genital NAAT is selected when appropriate.'),('Can I get tested after 3, 6 or 8 months?','Yes, but a negative NAAT months later cannot prove an infection never occurred.')],'gonorrhea-chlamydia-pcr hepatitis-b-triple-panel sti-testing-window-periods oral-throat-symptoms')
add('sti-testing-after-anal-sex','STI Testing After Anal Sex – Rectal Swab NAAT in Bengaluru','Anal exposure can require rectal or urethral site-specific testing depending on the type of contact, as well as HIV and other blood tests based on risk.','Rectal discharge, bleeding, pain or ulcers may require medical examination. QXL lists extragenital screening; confirm the assay’s suitability for anorectal specimens and collection requirements before booking.', [('Can urine PCR detect rectal gonorrhoea?','No. A urine test cannot exclude infection confined to the rectum.'),('What is three-site STI testing?','Testing relevant pharyngeal, rectal and urogenital sites. It is selected based on anatomy and exposure.'),('What if I have anal pain or bleeding?','Seek clinical assessment; severe pain or heavy bleeding warrants urgent care.')],'gonorrhea-chlamydia-pcr hiv-pep-prep-testing sti-testing-after-oral-sex')
add('sti-testing-after-vaginal-sex','STI Testing After Vaginal Sex – Blood, Urine and Genital Swabs','After vaginal intercourse, the appropriate specimens depend on anatomy and contact. A first-catch urine specimen or genital swab can be suitable for gonorrhoea/chlamydia NAAT; vaginal swabs are often preferred for vaginal/cervical screening.','Consider HIV, syphilis and hepatitis tests based on risk, and pregnancy assessment if relevant. A normal urine routine report does not rule out an STI.', [('Can infection occur without ejaculation?','Yes, depending on the infection and contact.'),('Do women need a urine test or vaginal swab?','The appropriate specimen depends on the assay and anatomy; a vaginal swab is often preferred for CT/NG screening.'),('Can I have an infection with no symptoms?','Yes. Discuss exposure-based screening.')],'gonorrhea-chlamydia-pcr pregnancy-and-sti-testing genital-discharge-and-burning')
add('asymptomatic-sti-screening','STI Screening Without Symptoms – Testing After a New Partner','Many STIs can be asymptomatic. Screening should be based on individual risk, anatomy, exposure sites, age, pregnancy and previous diagnosis, not a fixed large panel for everyone.','A new partner, multiple partners or a partner with a diagnosed infection can prompt evaluation. Discuss the need for repeat screening, vaccination and prevention with a clinician.', [('Do I need tests if I feel healthy?','Possibly. Symptoms are not required for infection; a clinician can select screening based on risk.'),('Can I screen before a new relationship?','Yes. Couples can discuss testing, relevant windows and vaccination.'),('Should I order every PCR target including Ureaplasma?','Not routinely. Many organisms and tests are inappropriate for asymptomatic screening.')],'couples-sti-screening sti-testing-after-unprotected-sex confidential-sti-testing-bangalore')
add('genital-discharge-and-burning','Penile Discharge, Burning Urine and Private-Part Pain After Sex','Clear, white, yellow or green discharge, urethral burning and pain with urination can occur with urethritis or other conditions including urinary infection. Color alone does not identify the cause.','Clinical assessment may include first-catch urine or suitable genital swab NAAT for gonorrhoea/chlamydia, urine culture where indicated and other tests for persistent symptoms. Severe testicular pain or swelling requires urgent care.', [('Is watery penile leakage always an STI?','No. It may have infectious or non-infectious causes; assessment is more reliable than appearance.'),('Can a normal urine routine rule out gonorrhoea?','No. Targeted NAAT is different from urinalysis.'),('What about persistent discharge after antibiotics?','Seek reassessment, including possible reinfection, resistance, Mycoplasma genitalium or non-infectious causes.')],'uti-vs-sti persistent-urethritis-testing gonorrhea-chlamydia-pcr')
add('genital-itching-and-irritation','Private-Part Itching, Redness, Burning and Genital Skin Irritation','Genital itching, redness, swelling, cracked skin and peeling may reflect fungal infection, dermatitis, balanitis, irritation or an STI. Symptoms after sex do not prove transmission.','Seek a clinician’s examination. Depending on findings, microscopy, culture or targeted molecular testing may be useful. Avoid applying harsh chemicals or self-medicating.', [('Is itching after sex always an STD?','No. Irritants, yeast and other skin conditions are common alternative causes.'),('Can red or white spots on the foreskin be diagnosed from a photo?','Appearance alone is often insufficient; examination and selected testing may be necessary.'),('Does a blood STI panel diagnose balanitis?','Not by itself. Local examination and specimen testing may be needed.')],'genital-sores-blisters-ulcers vaginal-discharge-testing genital-discharge-and-burning')
add('genital-sores-blisters-ulcers','Genital Sores, Blisters, Boils, Marks and Ulcers After Sex','Painful blisters, a painless ulcer, cuts, pimples, boils, scars or warts can have multiple infectious and non-infectious causes. A photograph or pain pattern does not establish a diagnosis.','A fresh lesion may be suitable for HSV PCR; syphilis serology and selected lesion testing may be indicated. Assess persistent or worsening lesions promptly; clinical examination is important.', [('Is a painless genital sore always syphilis?','No. Syphilis is one possibility and requires appropriate clinical and laboratory assessment.'),('Which test detects herpes from an active blister?','A lesion NAAT/PCR is preferred when a suitable active lesion is present.'),('Can a genital wart be diagnosed with a blood HPV test?','No routine blood HPV test establishes a wart diagnosis. Seek clinical assessment.')],'genital-herpes-testing syphilis-rpr-tpha-testing hpv-dna-testing')
add('vaginal-discharge-testing','Vaginal Discharge, Itching, Odour and Burning After Sex','Vaginal discharge can be physiological or related to yeast, bacterial vaginosis, trichomoniasis, cervicitis or other causes. Fishy smell, frothy or curd-like discharge does not establish a diagnosis.','Clinical assessment may include a vaginal swab for microscopy or targeted molecular testing, CT/NG NAAT and pregnancy-related evaluation where relevant. Severe pelvic pain, fever or pregnancy with bleeding warrants urgent assessment.', [('What causes white discharge and itching?','Candida is one possibility, but symptoms overlap with other conditions.'),('Is bacterial vaginosis the same as an STI?','No. It is an imbalance of vaginal flora, although sexual activity can influence risk.'),('Can a vaginal swab detect chlamydia and gonorrhoea?','A suitable validated NAAT can detect both; confirm the specimen and method.')],'uti-vs-sti gonorrhea-chlamydia-pcr trichomonas-testing')
add('oral-throat-symptoms','Sore Throat, Mouth Ulcers or White Tonsil Spots After Oral Sex','Sore throat, white patches, swollen glands, mouth sores and painful swallowing after oral sex have many possible causes, including common respiratory infections. A throat STI may also be asymptomatic.','Seek examination for persistent symptoms. A site-specific gonorrhoea NAAT may be appropriate after pharyngeal exposure. A regular throat culture is not automatically equivalent to STI NAAT. Difficulty breathing or swallowing saliva needs urgent care.', [('Can gonorrhoea look like tonsillitis?','Symptoms overlap; appropriate testing is needed.'),('Can negative urine PCR exclude throat infection?','No. Specimen site matters.'),('Is every sore throat after oral sex an STI?','No. Common throat infections and non-infectious causes are possible.')],'sti-testing-after-oral-sex gonorrhea-chlamydia-pcr genital-herpes-testing')
add('hiv-symptoms-and-anxiety','Fever, Rash, Swollen Glands or HIV Anxiety After Sex','Fever, fatigue, rash, ulcers, swollen glands, night sweats and body pain are nonspecific. HIV may be present without symptoms; symptoms cannot confirm or exclude infection.','Use an appropriate test and its window period. Repeated testing after an adequate negative result may not help persistent anxiety. Seek medical and counselling support rather than interpreting every symptom or test index as evidence of HIV.', [('Can anxiety cause physical symptoms?','Yes, but persistent or severe symptoms should be clinically assessed.'),('Can a normal CBC rule out HIV?','No. HIV-specific testing is necessary when indicated.'),('All my HIV tests are negative but I am afraid. What next?','Review the assay, exposure timing, medications and new exposures with a clinician; consider counselling if uncertainty persists.')],'hiv-testing-after-exposure negative-sti-test-persistent-symptoms sti-testing-window-periods')
add('uti-vs-sti','UTI vs STI – Burning Urine, Urethritis and Urine PCR Testing','Painful or frequent urination can be caused by UTI, STI-related urethritis or other conditions. Routine urine analysis and midstream urine culture address different questions from a first-catch urine STI NAAT.','A clinician may order both types of testing depending on symptoms. Follow collection instructions because midstream and first-catch specimens are not interchangeable.', [('Does urine culture detect chlamydia?','Routine urine culture does not reliably detect chlamydia; use an appropriate NAAT.'),('Why is urine culture negative but burning persists?','Possible causes include urethritis, prior treatment, irritation or other urinary disorders.'),('Can I submit one urine sample for every test?','Not necessarily. Ask the laboratory about separate containers and collection methods.')],'gonorrhea-chlamydia-pcr persistent-urethritis-testing genital-discharge-and-burning')
add('gonorrhea-chlamydia-pcr','Gonorrhoea and Chlamydia PCR / NAAT Testing in Bangalore','NAAT detects genetic material from Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis in appropriate specimens. Tests can be selected for urogenital, pharyngeal or rectal sites; the assay must be suitable for the specimen.','QXL lists combined CT/NG testing and extragenital screening. Confirm sample kit, site validation, collection instructions and fee. Gonorrhoea culture with susceptibility testing may be needed for suspected treatment failure.', [('Can blood tests diagnose local gonorrhoea or chlamydia?','Blood serology is not a substitute for site-specific NAAT for routine genital infection.'),('Which is better, urine or swab?','It depends on anatomy, site and the assay. Vaginal swabs are often preferred for screening in people with a vagina; first-catch urine is useful for urethral testing.'),('Can I test after antibiotics or repeat a PCR immediately after treatment?','Prior treatment and residual nucleic acid can affect results. Follow clinician-directed timing.'),('How do I know the assay is validated?','Request the manufacturer’s specimen claim, local verification and performing laboratory details.')],'sti-testing-after-oral-sex sti-testing-after-anal-sex sti-sample-collection')
add('syphilis-rpr-tpha-testing','Syphilis RPR, VDRL and TPHA Screening in Bangalore','Syphilis serology generally uses a non-treponemal test such as RPR or VDRL together with a treponemal test such as TPHA. Results require clinical context and previous treatment history.','A quantitative RPR titre may assist follow-up; early disease can be seronegative. Examination of sores or unexplained rash is important.', [('What does RPR reactive or a titre of 1:4 mean?','A titre is not a standalone diagnosis. Interpret with treponemal results, history and clinician assessment.'),('Why is TPHA positive but RPR negative?','Possible explanations include previously treated infection, early infection or other patterns requiring review.'),('Do treponemal tests remain positive after cure?','Often yes; they are not generally used to monitor treatment response.')],'genital-sores-blisters-ulcers understanding-sti-test-results')
add('hepatitis-b-triple-panel','Hepatitis B Triple Panel – HBsAg, Anti-HBs and Total Anti-HBc','The HBV triple panel comprises hepatitis B surface antigen, surface antibody and total core antibody. The combination helps distinguish infection, immunity and susceptibility. HBsAg alone is not the triple panel.','Confirm the availability and fee for all three components with QXL. Vaccination, exposure timing and additional tests may influence interpretation. A clinician can assess hepatitis B vaccine or immunoglobulin needs after relevant recent exposure.', [('Is total anti-HBc the same as IgM anti-HBc?','No. Total anti-HBc measures total core antibodies; IgM is a separate marker used in selected clinical situations.'),('Can I check hepatitis B immunity after vaccination?','Anti-HBs may help in indicated circumstances; interpret according to vaccination history and clinical context.'),('Do I need HBV DNA PCR after exposure?','Not routinely for everyone. It may be appropriate for selected diagnosis or management questions.')],'hepatitis-c-testing sti-testing-after-unprotected-sex understanding-sti-test-results')
add('hepatitis-c-testing','Hepatitis C Antibody and HCV RNA PCR After Sexual Exposure','Hepatitis C is primarily transmitted through blood; sexual transmission risk varies by circumstances. An HCV antibody test detects exposure, and HCV RNA testing assesses current infection.','A reactive antibody result requires RNA testing to determine current infection. Recent exposure can warrant RNA testing or follow-up despite negative antibodies.', [('Can HCV antibody be positive with RNA not detected?','Yes; this can reflect resolved infection or a false-reactive antibody result.'),('Can I get hepatitis C through sexual contact?','It is possible, particularly where blood exposure or specific risk factors are present.'),('Which HCV test is appropriate after recent exposure?','A clinician may select RNA testing and follow-up based on timing and risk.')],'hepatitis-b-triple-panel understanding-sti-test-results')
add('genital-herpes-testing','Genital and Oral Herpes Testing – HSV-1, HSV-2 Lesion PCR','When active sores or blisters are present, lesion NAAT/PCR can identify HSV. A type-specific IgG blood test has limitations and cannot reliably tell the location or date of infection.','Routine HSV serological screening of asymptomatic people is generally not recommended. HSV IgM is not recommended for diagnosis. Seek prompt clinical assessment while lesions are fresh.', [('Is HSV-1 IgG positive proof of genital herpes?','No. It does not reliably distinguish oral from genital infection.'),('What about borderline HSV-2 IgG?','False positives can occur; clinician-directed supplemental testing may be necessary.'),('Can herpes be diagnosed from a picture?','Appearance alone is unreliable; lesion testing and examination may be needed.')],'genital-sores-blisters-ulcers understanding-sti-test-results')
add('hpv-dna-testing','HPV DNA Testing, Genital Warts and Cervical Screening','HPV can spread by skin-to-skin sexual contact. Cervical HPV testing is intended for appropriate cervical screening or follow-up, not a universal test after a single sexual exposure.','Genital warts usually require clinical assessment. Do not assume a validated cervical HPV assay is appropriate for male, oral or rectal screening. Discuss vaccination and screening eligibility.', [('Is there a blood test for HPV?','No routine clinical blood test diagnoses genital HPV infection.'),('What is HPV DNA testing with Pap or LBC?','These assess cervical high-risk HPV and/or cervical cellular changes when indicated.'),('Can men get a routine HPV screening test?','There is no general routine HPV screening test for men; specific clinical contexts require specialist guidance.')],'genital-sores-blisters-ulcers pregnancy-and-sti-testing')
add('trichomonas-testing','Trichomonas vaginalis Testing – Urine or Vaginal Swab NAAT','Trichomoniasis can cause vaginal discharge or urethral symptoms and may be asymptomatic. Suitable molecular testing can detect the organism in approved specimens.','QXL lists a CT/NG/Trichomonas molecular profile. Confirm specimen suitability, particularly for male urine, before collection. A clinician determines indications and follow-up.', [('Does frothy discharge prove trichomoniasis?','No. Symptoms overlap with other vaginal infections.'),('Can urine routine detect trichomonas?','Routine microscopy is not a reliable substitute for appropriate targeted testing.'),('Can one PCR detect three pathogens?','Some multiplex assays detect chlamydia, gonorrhoea and trichomonas; confirm the platform and sample type.')],'vaginal-discharge-testing gonorrhea-chlamydia-pcr')
add('persistent-urethritis-testing','Persistent Urethritis and Mycoplasma genitalium Testing','Persistent or recurrent urethritis or cervicitis after initial evaluation may warrant Mycoplasma genitalium NAAT or other targeted testing, as clinically indicated.','Routine screening for asymptomatic Mycoplasma genitalium is not recommended; routine Ureaplasma or Mycoplasma hominis testing is generally not appropriate. Assess reinfection, treatment history and non-infectious causes.', [('Should I order Mycoplasma testing after every sexual exposure?','No. It is used in selected clinical scenarios.'),('What if CT/NG are negative but discharge continues?','Seek clinical assessment for other organisms, inflammation and non-infectious causes.'),('Does detecting Ureaplasma prove disease?','No. Detection may represent colonization and does not necessarily require treatment.')],'negative-sti-test-persistent-symptoms genital-discharge-and-burning')
add('other-genital-infections','Other Genital Infections – Candida, BV, Dermatitis and Less Common Causes','Genital symptoms are not synonymous with STIs. Candida, bacterial vaginosis, dermatitis, scabies and other conditions may cause itching or lesions. Less common infections such as LGV, chancroid, mpox or donovanosis may require specialist evaluation.','Target testing to clinical findings, anatomy and epidemiology. Confirm whether specific cultures or molecular assays are available; do not assume every organism is included in an STI package.', [('Does Candida always mean sexual transmission?','No. Yeast infections can occur without sexual transmission.'),('Can genital boils be non-sexually transmitted?','Yes. Folliculitis and other skin conditions are possible.'),('Can a broad multiplex result diagnose every genital symptom?','No. Examination and clinical context remain essential.')],'genital-itching-and-irritation vaginal-discharge-testing genital-sores-blisters-ulcers')
add('sti-sample-collection','STI Specimen Collection – Blood, First-Catch Urine, Throat and Genital Swabs','The correct specimen varies with the investigation and the anatomical site. Follow the laboratory’s kit-specific instructions; a urine specimen intended for routine culture is not automatically appropriate for STI NAAT.','Many serology tests do not require fasting. Confirm urine volume, time since urination, swab medium and transport. Some swabs require a trained clinician; self-collection is appropriate only where the assay and workflow support it.', [('Can I eat or drink before STI blood tests?','Fasting is generally unnecessary for the tests discussed, unless another selected investigation requires it.'),('Can I use first-morning or midstream urine?','Follow the exact test instruction; first-catch urine means the initial urine stream, not necessarily first morning.'),('Can I collect a swab at home?','Only if the specific assay and laboratory workflow support it.'),('Does mouthwash, menstruation or recent intercourse affect collection?','Requirements vary by assay. Confirm before collecting and disclose relevant medications or symptoms.')],'gonorrhea-chlamydia-pcr sti-home-sample-collection')
add('sti-testing-after-antibiotics','STI Testing After Antibiotics, PEP or Previous Treatment','Antibiotics can alter organism detection and residual nucleic acid can sometimes remain after treatment. HIV prevention medication can influence HIV detection windows.','Tell the clinician the actual medicine, dates and doses. Distinguish test of cure from retesting for reinfection; avoid self-medication and unscheduled repeat testing.', [('Can antibiotics make STI PCR negative?','Treatment can reduce detectable organisms; interpretation depends on timing and organism.'),('Can PCR stay positive after treatment?','Yes, some NAATs may detect residual nucleic acid; follow pathogen-specific guidance.'),('Can both partners need evaluation?','Yes. Partner assessment may be necessary to prevent reinfection.')],'persistent-urethritis-testing hiv-pep-prep-testing understanding-sti-test-results')
add('negative-sti-test-persistent-symptoms','Negative STI Tests But Symptoms Persist – Next Steps','A negative result answers a particular question for a specific specimen, method and time. It may not exclude infection at another site, a condition not included in the panel, or non-infectious causes.','Review the exact report and exposure with a clinician rather than repeating broad panels indefinitely. Persistent genital pain, discharge or lesions warrant examination.', [('Can a urine PCR miss a throat or rectal infection?','Yes. Anatomical site is important.'),('Can every genital symptom be caused by an STI?','No. Dermatological, urinary and other conditions can have similar symptoms.'),('Do I need repeated negative HIV tests?','Review the window and any new exposure or antiretroviral use with a clinician; unnecessary repeats may worsen anxiety.')],'uti-vs-sti hiv-symptoms-and-anxiety persistent-urethritis-testing')
add('understanding-sti-test-results','Understanding STI Reports – Reactive, Non-Reactive, Detected and Not Detected','Screening results, quantitative titres, antibody index values and molecular Ct values have different meanings. Interpretation depends on the assay and clinical history.','A reactive HIV screening result is not a confirmed diagnosis. A positive treponemal result may reflect treated syphilis. HCV antibody requires RNA assessment for current infection. A laboratory report should be reviewed with a clinician.', [('What is HIV S/CO or COI value?','It is an assay-specific signal or index used against its cutoff; it is not a viral load or measure of infection risk.'),('Does PCR Ct tell how infectious I am?','Not reliably by itself; Ct depends on assay and specimen, and should not be used as a standalone measure of infectiousness.'),('What does not detected mean?','The target was not detected in the tested specimen under assay conditions; timing and specimen quality matter.'),('Should I repeat an unexpected reactive result?','Follow the recommended supplemental testing algorithm with a clinician.')],'hiv-testing-after-exposure syphilis-rpr-tpha-testing hepatitis-b-triple-panel')
add('couples-sti-screening','Couples, Premarital and New-Partner STI Screening in Bangalore','Couples may choose risk-based screening before a new relationship or pregnancy. Results depend on each person’s exposure and timing, and one partner’s result does not establish the other’s status.','Discuss consent, vaccination, test selection and window periods. If a partner has a confirmed STI, seek clinician assessment; partner management may be needed.', [('Can one partner be positive and the other negative?','Yes. Transmission and detection vary.'),('Can I ask for my partner’s report?','Reports are confidential and should be shared only with appropriate authorization and legal requirements.'),('Is every STI panel necessary before marriage?','No. Select tests through risk assessment and shared decision-making.')],'asymptomatic-sti-screening pregnancy-and-sti-testing confidential-sti-testing-bangalore')
add('pregnancy-and-sti-testing','Pregnancy Testing and STI Screening After Unprotected Sex','Pregnancy and STI risk are distinct. If pregnancy is possible after recent unprotected intercourse, emergency contraception advice is time-sensitive. A urine or serum hCG test may be useful at appropriate timing.','Seek antenatal guidance for HIV, syphilis, hepatitis B and other indicated testing according to local protocols. Severe pelvic pain or pregnancy with bleeding needs urgent evaluation.', [('Can I test for pregnancy seven days after sex?','A test this early may miss pregnancy. Follow clinician or test instructions and repeat if appropriate.'),('Can emergency contraception prevent STIs?','No. It does not prevent infection.'),('Which STI tests are appropriate during pregnancy?','Antenatal protocols and individual risk determine tests; discuss with an obstetric clinician.')],'urgent-medical-support couples-sti-screening')
add('sti-and-fertility','STIs, Fertility and Reproductive Health Investigations','Untreated chlamydia and gonorrhoea can contribute to pelvic inflammatory disease and fertility problems. Genital symptoms or infertility have many other causes.','Seek appropriate gynecology, urology or fertility assessment. STI NAAT, semen testing and other investigations answer different questions and are chosen clinically.', [('Does a negative STI test rule out past complications?','No. A current negative infection test cannot exclude a prior cleared infection or resulting damage.'),('Can a semen culture diagnose every STI?','No. Targeted specimen-specific methods may be needed.'),('What about screening before IVF?','Follow the fertility centre’s clinical and regulatory requirements.')],'couples-sti-screening pregnancy-and-sti-testing')
add('urgent-medical-support','Urgent Medical Help After Sexual Exposure or Sexual Assault','Possible HIV exposure within 72 hours, recent sexual assault, severe pelvic or testicular pain, heavy bleeding or serious systemic symptoms need prompt medical attention. A laboratory booking must never delay care.','Seek a qualified emergency clinician. After non-consensual contact, medical support, prevention, forensic options, consent and counselling can be discussed without first purchasing a test. If immediate safety is at risk, contact local emergency services.', [('Do I need to wait for a laboratory test before seeking help?','No. Seek care now; the clinician can arrange testing and prevention.'),('Can emergency contraception be considered after assault?','Yes, when relevant and time-sensitive; a qualified clinician can discuss options.'),('Can I get care without sharing my history publicly?','Yes. Discuss confidentiality and consent privately with the medical team.')],'hiv-pep-prep-testing pregnancy-and-sti-testing')
add('confidential-sti-testing-bangalore','Confidential STD, STI and HIV Testing in Bengaluru','QXL Diagnostics lists sexual health investigations at its Bengaluru laboratory network. Request a private discussion of suitable tests, sample types, itemised fees and report delivery before booking.','Confidential does not mean automatically anonymous or exempt from medical identification and reporting requirements. Ask about consent, identity requirements, secure report access and applicable exceptions. People of all genders and relationship statuses deserve respectful care.', [('Can I get tested without telling my family or employer?','Ask the laboratory about its confidentiality policy and applicable legal requirements. Do not assume absolute anonymity.'),('Can I get tested without a prescription or Aadhaar?','Requirements vary by investigation and applicable policy; confirm before booking.'),('Is QXL a place to ask about discreet testing?','Yes, call the laboratory to request a private enquiry and confirm collection and report delivery options.'),('Which collection locations are available?','QXL lists Kengeri and Yelahanka. Confirm which facility can collect the specimen you need.')],'sti-home-sample-collection sti-test-cost-bangalore sti-testing-after-unprotected-sex')
add('sti-test-cost-bangalore','STI and STD Test Cost in Bangalore – Individual Tests and Packages','Total cost depends on the selected investigations, specimen sites, collection charges and any reflex or supplementary tests. A large panel is not necessarily more appropriate than targeted screening.','QXL publicly lists fourth-generation HIV screening at ₹945 and a 6-hour turnaround; confirm current fee and TAT. Other prices and availability should come from the approved laboratory catalogue, not estimates.', [('How much is a full STD panel?','Request an itemised quotation specifying tests, specimen types, reflex fees and turnaround.'),('Can I get a same-day HIV test?','QXL lists a 6-hour laboratory turnaround for its fourth-generation screen; confirm collection cutoff and actual reporting time.'),('Is a throat or rectal PCR included in a blood-and-urine panel?','Not automatically. Check the package components and specimen types.')],'confidential-sti-testing-bangalore sti-sample-collection sti-testing-after-oral-sex')
add('sti-home-sample-collection','Confidential STI Home Sample Collection in Bangalore','Home blood collection and eligible urine collection may be available. Procedural throat, genital, rectal or lesion swabs can require a clinician and a suitable collection site.','Confirm postcode coverage, specimen-specific suitability, fee, preparation and secure result delivery. Home collection is not the same as an unverified self-test.', [('Can a phlebotomist collect blood for HIV screening at home?','QXL lists home collection for eligible blood testing; confirm availability in your area.'),('Can I collect a throat or rectal STI swab at home?','Do not assume so; confirm the assay and approved collection protocol.'),('Can I book by phone?','Call QXL at +91 99646 39639 to request a private enquiry.')],'sti-sample-collection confidential-sti-testing-bangalore')
add('sti-risk-kissing-and-genital-contact','STI Risk From Kissing, Genital Rubbing and Non-Penetrative Sex','STI risk depends on the contact. HIV does not spread through casual touching or saliva; ordinary kissing does not transmit HIV. Herpes, HPV and some other infections can spread through direct contact with infected skin or lesions.','Do not assume every intimate activity requires a full STI panel. Discuss any actual blood exposure, open sores, shared sex toys or mucosal contact with a clinician. Avoid sharing sex toys without appropriate cleaning and protection.', [('Can kissing transmit HIV?','Ordinary kissing does not transmit HIV. Seek advice for unusual blood exposure rather than routine testing for kissing alone.'),('Can an STI spread without penetration or ejaculation?','Yes, certain infections spread through skin or mucosal contact.'),('Does touching semen or genital fluids require an HIV test?','Intact skin contact does not transmit HIV. A clinician can assess contact with mucosa or damaged skin if relevant.')],'sti-testing-after-oral-sex sti-testing-after-unprotected-sex asymptomatic-sti-screening')
add('inclusive-sexual-health-testing','Inclusive and Confidential Sexual Health Testing in Bengaluru','Sexual health testing should be respectful and based on anatomy, exposure sites, symptoms and individual risk, not assumptions about sexual orientation, gender identity, marital status or occupation.','People with new or multiple partners, LGBTQ+ people, students and unmarried couples may request a private discussion of suitable tests. Confirm the laboratory’s confidentiality and collection policies; arrange clinician-directed screening where appropriate.', [('Can I request testing regardless of gender or relationship status?','Discuss individualized testing confidentially; applicable consent and identification requirements still apply.'),('What is site-specific testing for men who have sex with men?','Depending on exposure, urethral, rectal and pharyngeal screening may be indicated; clinician guidance determines timing.'),('Can testing be non-judgmental and discreet?','You may request a private enquiry, ask about report delivery and confirm confidentiality practices before booking.')],'confidential-sti-testing-bangalore asymptomatic-sti-screening sti-testing-after-anal-sex')
